00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:55.350
You're so talented.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:56.620
You have an album out, you have...

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.110
And it's like your seventh album.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.020
You work on TV, you do
movies, you do everything.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.620
You're like one of those people
that likes to do everything.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.260
So you, do you have to keep busy?

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.596
Do you like doing that? Do you get bored?

00:01:05.596 --> 00:01:07.510
- Yeah, I mean, I must be
compensating for something.

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:08.830
Like, there must be some deep-seeded...

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.090
- [Kelly] I'm in therapy,
just so you know...

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.547
- Sense of insecurity that I'm,

00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:12.380
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.040
I'm, I'm just trying
to erase from my mind.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:14.873
- [Kelly] I know.

00:01:14.873 --> 00:01:15.706
They're like, "Why do
you like to be so busy?"

00:01:15.706 --> 00:01:16.540
And I'm like, "I don't want to stop

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.078
and be still and notice what's happening".

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:18.911
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:18.911 --> 00:01:19.744
- Yeah, that's, that could be part of it.

00:01:19.744 --> 00:01:21.260
- [Kelly] Yes, it's probably true.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.040
So wait, one of the things
that you're promoting,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:23.970
is your seventh album.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.310
So, what sets this one apart?

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.410
Because I feel like every
album I've come out with,

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:29.339
it's very different, I'm
in a very different place.

00:01:29.339 --> 00:01:30.172
- [Seth] Yeah.

00:01:30.172 --> 00:01:31.005
- [Kelly] So, what is this one?

00:01:31.005 --> 00:01:32.019
- [Seth] That's a nice
candid photo, I had no idea.

00:01:32.019 --> 00:01:33.478
- [Kelly] I love it.

00:01:33.478 --> 00:01:34.520
(audience chuckles)
I love it.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:35.353
- It's, it's...

00:01:35.353 --> 00:01:36.233
- You like classic songs, I love that.

00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:37.240
- [Seth] I do, I do.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.820
I love, I love orchestras,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.163
I love the sound of great arrangements.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.730
Great orchestrator, Andrew Cottee,

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.150
terrific producer, Joel McNeely,

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:48.400
who writes a lot of music for me,

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.320
both worked on this album.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.740
It's, it's an uptempo, swing album.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:57.170
That's, that should, should
make people feel pretty happy.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:58.780
- Yeah, that's what I
love about this show.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:01:59.920
You get to do different songs.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:00.753
- [Seth] Yep, yep.

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:02.070
And, but I think that's what I love.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.450
I think you, like Robbie
Williams has done it a bit,

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.830
like, it's like, I like that
people do that throwback sound.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.112
It's, I mean, God, Amy
Winehouse made a whole career...

00:02:09.112 --> 00:02:09.945
- Absolutely, yeah.

00:02:09.945 --> 00:02:10.950
- Off of like, doing a
throwback vibe to that.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.070
So, it's really cool.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:13.670
I don't think enough people appreciate,

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.430
those orchestral arrangements
and how beautiful it is.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:17.590
- Yeah, well I mean you,

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:19.937
you listen to the Sinatra ballad albums,

00:02:19.937 --> 00:02:22.000
like "Only the Lonely"
and "Wee Small Hours".

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.420
And, and you know, as great
a vocalist as he was even,

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:25.253
you take the vocals out of it

00:02:25.253 --> 00:02:26.720
and you listen to what's going on.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:27.553
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:27.553 --> 00:02:28.386
- With the orchestra,

00:02:28.386 --> 00:02:29.500
there's really nothing like that today.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.333
- [Kelly] It's beautiful.

00:02:30.333 --> 00:02:32.180
One of my favorite songs of
all time is "Somethin' Stupid".

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.450
Like, I love you, he
did with Nancy Sinatra.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:34.441
- Sure, sure, yeah.

00:02:34.441 --> 00:02:35.357
- So, if you ever cover
that song and you're like,

00:02:35.357 --> 00:02:37.480
"Who will sing this with me?", I will.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:38.313
- Okay, all right, all right.
(audience applauds)

00:02:38.313 --> 00:02:40.316
- [Kelly] Because I love that song.

00:02:40.316 --> 00:02:41.227
(audience applauds)

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.940
And I listen, I listen to
Sinatra all time, I love it.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.930
It's just such a peaceful
vibe and it's so pretty.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.780
And it, I get, I think
old music like that too,

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.440
like older music gives space.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.410
It gives space to breathe.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:52.136
And now we're trying to fill
all the moments, you know?

00:02:52.136 --> 00:02:54.310
- It does, and it's, and
it's, and it's roboto,

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.260
which means there's not always a tempo.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.160
- [Kelly] I know.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.320
- The orchestra and the
singer in the same room, yeah.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.710
- I have my own tempo.
- Yeah.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:02.710
- This is a joke in my
band for like 20 years.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:03.543
(Seth chuckles)

00:03:03.543 --> 00:03:04.448
They're like, "This is Kelly time".

00:03:04.448 --> 00:03:05.281
(audience laughs)

00:03:05.281 --> 00:03:06.317
It's like, because I'm,
I'm, it's like singer time.

00:03:06.317 --> 00:03:08.000
It's like I just want to hold this out

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:08.840
as long as I want to,

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:10.630
and then, come back when I want to.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:11.463
- Yeah.
(audience applauds)

00:03:11.463 --> 00:03:13.320
- You've recently announced
that you're doing a TV series,

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.776
based on the movie "Ted",
which everybody loves Ted.

00:03:15.776 --> 00:03:16.609
(audience applauds)
- [Seth] Yes, yes, yeah.

00:03:16.609 --> 00:03:17.442
- [Kelly] It's so funny.

00:03:17.442 --> 00:03:18.275
(audience applauds)

00:03:18.275 --> 00:03:21.830
So, what made you want to
do the TV series of it?

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.820
- It helped that the studio asked.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:24.653
- [Kelly] Yeah, that's good.
(audience laughs)

00:03:24.653 --> 00:03:25.530
People are interested.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.010
- That was a, that was a helpful,

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:28.610
that was a good place to begin.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:29.550
- But do you get nervous about that?

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.270
Because it's such a pop
culture, it's such a huge thing.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:32.150
Do you get nervous?

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:34.230
- You know, it's a combination of nerves,

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.330
because we set a pretty high bar with the,

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.728
with the films and,

00:03:37.728 --> 00:03:39.410
and you know, but at the same time,

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:41.576
it's, it's a comfortable space to work in.

00:03:41.576 --> 00:03:42.409
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:42.409 --> 00:03:45.318
- And it's a prequel,
it takes place in 1993.

00:03:45.318 --> 00:03:48.000
So, we're kind of digging back into the...

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:48.833
- [Kelly] I love it.

00:03:48.833 --> 00:03:50.100
- Pop culture of that era.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:50.933
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:03:50.933 --> 00:03:52.770
Okay, did you have any
favorite like Stuffies

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.363
or toys when you were a kid?

00:03:54.363 --> 00:03:56.100
Mine were Care Bears.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:56.933
- [Seth] Care bears?

00:03:56.933 --> 00:03:57.766
- Loved care bears.

00:03:57.766 --> 00:03:58.599
- [Seth] You liked the Care Bears.

00:03:58.599 --> 00:03:59.432
- Wouldn't have you liked to have seen

00:03:59.432 --> 00:04:01.000
a care bear speak like Ted?
(Kelly laughs)

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.170
- Yeah, yeah.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:03.430
That, that was the whole
idea behind the movie.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:04.481
- [Kelly] Sunshine Bear, yeah.

00:04:04.481 --> 00:04:06.420
- But the whole, the
whole theory behind Ted,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.830
was let's ruin everything.
(audience laughs)

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.567
- [Kelly] Let's ruin
Care Bears for everyone.

00:04:09.567 --> 00:04:11.540
(audience and Seth laugh)

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:12.623
But anyway, is it true that you built...

00:04:12.623 --> 00:04:15.920
Because I love, I'm a
Treky, like next generation,

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.700
like Jean-Luc Picard Worf,
I loved "Star Trek" so much.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.090
And I heard that you built a replica,

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:23.060
when you were a kid of,
did you do the Enterprise?

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:24.570
- I did, you know, my sister went,

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.480
went off to Japan as an exchange student

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.030
and her room was vacant for the summer.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:29.550
So, I was like, "Oh, this
would be kind of fun".

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:30.530
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.280
- Yeah, I think I built the, built the...

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.148
She went to Japan, yeah.

00:04:33.148 --> 00:04:33.981
- She got to go to Japan.

00:04:33.981 --> 00:04:34.814
- [Seth] Yeah, she did.

00:04:34.814 --> 00:04:35.647
- Did you get to do anything like that?

00:04:35.647 --> 00:04:36.480
- I didn't, I didn't.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.010
I would've loved, I still have never,

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.630
that's one place I've
never been, is Japan.

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:40.463
I'd love to go.

00:04:40.463 --> 00:04:41.360
- I love Japan.
- Yep.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.193
- Yeah.
- Yep.

00:04:42.193 --> 00:04:43.200
- The temples are really cool.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.033
- Yeah, people love it.

00:04:44.033 --> 00:04:44.940
- Because the city's so crowded,

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:46.950
but you get to the temples
and it's like so beautiful.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.620
- Yeah, and the cell phones
are like the size of like,

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:50.890
you know, they're like that big.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:51.830
- Oh, I don't...
- It's like the future.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:53.890
- I love that, I love
anything that's futuristic.

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:55.308
- I don't know, somebody,
somebody told me,

00:04:55.308 --> 00:04:56.141
I don't know if that's true.

00:04:56.141 --> 00:04:56.974
(audience laughs)

00:04:56.974 --> 00:04:57.807
- Oh, I was like, "I don't
remember the cell phone moment,

00:04:57.807 --> 00:04:59.460
but maybe you'll have yours".
(Seth laughs)

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:00.887
No, but I, I love that you built it,

00:05:00.887 --> 00:05:02.971
was it like cardboard or
like, you built it like?

00:05:02.971 --> 00:05:05.550
- Yeah, it was like cardboard
and Christmas lights.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:06.420
- [Kelly] Love.
(Seth laughs)

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:07.630
Who was your favorite character?

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:09.330
Like, Lieutenant Riker, I know them all.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:10.790
- My, my favorite, you know...

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:12.470
- Geordi La Forge, reading rainbow guy.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.400
- I, I loved them all.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.900
Jonathan Frakes is, is the
best guy to have a beer with,

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:17.733
I'll tell you that much.
(audience chuckles)

00:05:17.733 --> 00:05:18.566
- [Kelly] Okay, okay.

00:05:18.566 --> 00:05:19.399
I wouldn't know that,

00:05:19.399 --> 00:05:20.440
but I'm going to take your word on that.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:21.273
- [Seth] Highly recommend it.

00:05:21.273 --> 00:05:23.230
- Okay, sounds like a fun time

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:24.651
or maybe one that I might be scared of.

00:05:24.651 --> 00:05:25.549
(audience and Seth laugh)

00:05:25.549 --> 00:05:26.500
But you're, let's talk about it though.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.960
Because your, your show "The
Orville" it's set in space.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:29.820
Did you do that on purpose?

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.670
Is this like a, kind of a tribute to that?

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:32.503
That you loved it?

00:05:32.503 --> 00:05:33.520
- [Seth] Yeah, you know,
what what's interesting is,

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.140
you know when I was, and you know,

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:38.410
you, you are talking about
a specific genre of sci-fi,

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:39.620
you know, it, it, at the time,

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:43.920
there was a very optimistic,
forward-looking viewpoint

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.110
from the creators of sci-fi and Hollywood.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:48.480
And that's kind of disappeared
in the wake of, you know,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.470
things like "The Hunger
Games" and, and whatnot.

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:52.531
That kind of show us, you know...

00:05:52.531 --> 00:05:53.840
- If you don't win, you die.
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:54.840
- [Seth] If you don't win, you die.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.910
A cautionary tale and
what was kind of missing,

00:05:57.910 --> 00:05:59.560
was the kind of blueprint

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.360
for what we could have if we succeeded.

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:03.520
No one was really doing that.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:05.090
They hadn't really done
it since the nineties.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.860
So, that's kind of why we
started doing "The Orville".

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:09.670
It's like, well, this is a
great space for us to live in,

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:10.930
because nobody's going there right now.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:11.763
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:11.763 --> 00:06:12.779
- [Seth] And that's kind
of, that's kind of the,

00:06:12.779 --> 00:06:14.140
sort of the mission statement of the show.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.430
- [Kelly] I, I love that
and I love all those.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.230
I like "Star Trek",
"Battlestar Galactica",

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:19.572
all those things.

00:06:19.572 --> 00:06:20.405
I love like, anything futuristic and like,

00:06:20.405 --> 00:06:23.200
just the stories that come
into that, I'm such a nerd.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.010
I've never like, been to
like Comic-Con recently,

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:26.747
but I would go, but none
of my friends will go.

00:06:26.747 --> 00:06:29.910
So, how important, how
important is it for you

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:31.610
to get the science right on "The Orville"?

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:32.710
I feel like you're one of those guys

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:33.740
that doesn't want to get it wrong.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:35.680
- [Seth] Yeah, it's,
it's, it's very important.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.710
I think people watching medical shows,

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.840
or forensic shows or space shows,

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.330
think that Hollywood writers

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.101
have done all their homework all the time.

00:06:45.101 --> 00:06:45.934
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:45.934 --> 00:06:47.180
- Which is not always the case.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.460
In fact, there's a wonderful organization,

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:51.360
called the Science and
Entertainment Exchange,

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:52.970
that was started by Jerry and Janet Zucker

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:55.430
that connects Hollywood
writers with scientists,

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:57.051
that's how I met Neil deGrasse Tyson.

00:06:57.051 --> 00:06:58.010
- Oh, that's how you get it right?

00:06:58.010 --> 00:06:59.940
- [Seth] Yeah, and, and
so you pick up the phone

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.280
and you say, "Hey, this"...

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.180
Because it's in the
interest of both parties.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:04.840
You know, scientists want proper science

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:08.530
to be communicated to the public

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.450
and entertainment writers
don't want to look like idiots.

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:14.700
So, it's, it kind of works out both ways.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.310
So yeah, we have a, we
have a science consultant,

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.280
André Bormanis, who actually consulted

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:19.780
for "Star Trek" for many years,

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:21.260
who works on "The Orville".

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:22.990
If we get really stumped,
we'll pick up the phone,

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:23.823
we'll call Neil.

00:07:23.823 --> 00:07:25.254
- Yeah, I love Neil.

00:07:25.254 --> 00:07:26.683
- [Seth] He's great, he's great.

00:07:26.683 --> 00:07:28.130
- Now, he is a guy that
like, you have a talk with

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:29.540
and you either leave like,

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:31.040
so excited that you learned something

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.370
or we're all going to die.
(audience chuckles)

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:33.203
- Yeah, yeah.

00:07:33.203 --> 00:07:35.030
- [Kelly] Like, one time
we had a conversation

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:36.870
about black holes and I
was like, "Oh, my God".

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:38.000
Like, yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.130
I, the last time I had lunch with Neil,

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:42.470
I left feeling a whole lot smarter

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:44.131
and noticing that my fries were gone.

00:07:44.131 --> 00:07:45.910
(audience and Kelly laugh)

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:47.841
- You were just like...
(Kelly laughs)

00:07:47.841 --> 00:07:48.674
It's a distraction.

00:07:48.674 --> 00:07:51.003
- He knows what I'm talking
about, he knows what happened.

